<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gospel of John</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 90–110 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gospel of John is the fourth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical gospels</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, probably written around 90–110 CE. Its account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> differs from the other three gospels, with long discourses and a high Christology that opens with the Word (Logos) made flesh. Early Christian tradition linked it to the apostle John and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, where he was said to have lived in old age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john.jpg" alt="Gospel of John" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RylandsImaging · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JRL19060215_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
